From 12acf22640bd85dfe670ac912d56c01754508732 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: overdoingism <overdoingism@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 14:44:43 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] GDN verify v3: anchored rollback with replay
 (LLAMA_RS_ANCHOR), non-temporal hints, moved cells keep their snapshots

v3 keeps v1 (the 128-wide verify kernel loads the input state and stores the per-token snapshots non-temporally;
GGML_CUDA_GDN_NT=0, or GGML_LF_ALL=0 with it unset: plain loads and stores) and adds two changes; it is v2 with the
anchored rollback on by default.

Anchored rollback, on unless LLAMA_RS_ANCHOR=0 (unset, GGML_LF_ALL=0 turns it off; qwen35 / qwen35moe, f32 state; a
recurrent layer on a device other than CPU / CUDA / ROCm / MUSA turns it off).  A verify step no longer keeps one full
recurrent state per drafted token (1 + n_rs_seq planes per cell): each cell keeps two planes - the state after its last
batch and the state that batch started from - and the per-token replay factors of that batch (two factor rows per cell, by
parity).  A partial accept is replayed from the start state when the cell is next read (new ops ggml_gated_delta_net_anchor,
_anchor_rows and _replay, CPU and CUDA; the CUDA verify kernel writes the cache directly with non-temporal stores and reads
the factor rows by id), and a state read with a rollback pending replays it first.  A batch longer than a factor row
(n_rs_batch tokens) keeps no factors, the bound rollbacks already have.  The mechanism follows radiance's GDN verify
(Apache-2.0); the code is new.
gfx1201 (R9700), Qwen3.8-27B UD-Q4_K_XL + DFlash2: VRAM after loading 30.00 -> 28.97 GiB (2 slots, -c 245760), 24.69 -> 24.18
GiB (1 slot, -c 131072); Qwen3.6-35B-A3B 2 slots -0.42 GiB.  ms per step -0.39 % at 8K, -0.34 % at 110K; two slots decoding at
once +2.3 to +4.0 %.  Outputs are the same with the anchor on and off: test-backend-ops (GDN 101/101, replay 38/38), XL and
A3B server runs (text and draft counts), state save and restore, 2 to 4 sequences with moved and swapped cells.

Moved cells keep their rollback snapshots, with or without the anchor.  From 3 cells on, find_slot can move a cell out of the
way of a later ubatch of the same decode after it verified a draft; build_rs copied only plane 0, so a rollback of that
sequence read the snapshots another sequence had left at the new row and the output was wrong from then on (anchored: the
rollback was refused, and the server aborts on that).  build_rs now also copies planes 1..n_rs_seq of the moved states
(s_copy_extra), gathered before the ubatch writes its snapshots; anchored, the start state and the factor row move too.  With
1 or 2 cells no cell is moved this way and the graph is unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
